{"name":"Bonesmashing","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":215,"casual":{"lead":["Bonesmashing means striking facial bones, often with a hammer, in the hope of remodeling them into a sharper shape. It is a bonehacks practice in looksmaxxing, and Wikipedia says it is rarely actually done.","No clinical study has tested it. Surgeons have published letters warning about it as a social-media trend."],"sections":[{"h":"How it works","paras":["Believers say repeated impact stimulates bone to remodel. This mechanism has not been studied in the context of facial aesthetics."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks and caveats","paras":["Surgeons cite fractures, misalignment, neurovascular injury, deformity and vision changes as possible outcomes."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Bone smashing by late Miocene Hominidae.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5650960/","source":"Nature","year":1968,"cites":5,"kind":null},{"title":"Urgent concern regarding \"bone smashing\", a dangerous trend on TikTok.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38278441/","source":"Journal of stomatology, oral and maxillofacial surgery","year":2024,"cites":2,"kind":null},{"title":"Dangerous trends on social media related to facial esthetics: Analysis of looksmaxxing and its softmaxxing and hardmaxxing practices.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42674114/","source":"Journal of stomatology, oral and maxillofacial surgery","year":2026,"cites":0,"kind":null},{"title":"Risks of 'bone smashing' trend on social media.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38757990/","source":"Nursing","year":2024,"cites":0,"kind":null},{"title":"The bone smashing and the excesses of social networking trends.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38458546/","source":"Journal of stomatology, oral and maxillofacial surgery","year":2024,"cites":0,"kind":null},{"title":"Production device of steamed bone meal","url":"https://europepmc.org/article/PAT/CN201252805Y","source":null,"year":2009,"cites":0,"kin